{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q019_stable_periodic_release_semantics","answer":"The current-release endpoint (/api/v1/releases/current) reports SCOPe 2.08 as the stable base release. Its stable_label is "2.08-stable", its freeze date is 2021-07-28 and its release date is 2021-09-22. The endpoint marks it as public (is_public true) and not comprehensive (is_comprehensive false). The latest periodic update it reports is "2.08-2026-08-16", dated 2026-08-16. /api/v1/releases/2.08 returns the same metadata.

Which identifier to cite: for a reproducible analysis, cite the stable release, SCOPe 2.08 (label "2.08-stable"), and send release-scoped requests to /api/v1/releases/2.08/... with that release named explicitly. Do not rely on "current", because it will move when a newer release appears. Also note the access date and the periodic label you saw (2.08-2026-08-16) as provenance, but do not treat that label as the citable version. The OpenAPI spec says that base releases such as 2.08 can receive periodic updates and that "dated selectors do not promise immutable archived snapshots." So 2.08-2026-08-16 may not be retrievable in the same form later.

What the periodic label means: it names a dated incremental update applied on top of base release 2.08 (format "<base>-<YYYY-MM-DD>"). Such updates typically add newer PDB entries, often classified automatically. It is not a new stable release, and it does not re-curate the 2.08 hierarchy (class, fold, superfamily, family). The evolutionary meaning of the hierarchy levels comes from the stable 2.08 classification. Domains added in a periodic update are only as reliable as the automated method that assigned them, so results that depend on them should say which periodic state was used. If exact replication matters, save the domain or sequence data you actually used, rather than counting on the dated selector staying the same.","evidence_used":["/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/","/api/v1/releases/2.08","/api/v1/openapi.yaml"]}]}
